Whoa, hold your horses! We're on the pitch, not debugging a server room! But hey, if you're talking about getting a program to the final whistle, I've got you covered.

In soccer, you don't just stop running; you execute a clean play to end the match. In C, you use the `exit()` function from the `stdlib.h` library. It's like blowing the final whistle and sending everyone straight to the locker room. You can pass a status code—usually `0` for a clean win (success) or `1` for a loss (error)—to tell the referee (the operating system) how the game went.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // Do your plays here
    // ...

    // Time to go!
    exit(0); // 0 means we won the match!
}
```

Or, if you're in the middle of a play and need to cut the game short immediately, you can just return a value from `main()`. That's like a player getting a red card and being sent off instantly.

```c
int main() {
    if (something_went_wrong) {
        return 1; // Red card! Game over.
    }
    return 0; // Full time, we won!
}
```

